C} \\\\  & =4b-4a.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $c=\\lambda a+4b=-4a+4b.$\n\nSince $a∦b,$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients of $a,$ we get:\n\n$$\n\\lambda =-4\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235569 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1682cda8-7f11-5f06-82c3-3765e21a1e2b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234574}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1682cda8-7f11-5f06-82c3-3765e21a1e2b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234574} :answer-field/id #uuid "b5c917d1-2868-5524-8121-f0ecb3beaff1" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235194}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "57bb167a-2b83-56b4-8eae-feba2b6c609a" :question/math-academy-id "q-367059" :question/problem "![](images/9d/9d117a1429a5d5264ca08bfae899086d821b37a84ec03921347f8e0fb207343f.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. If $c=\\lambda a+2b,$ then$\\,\\lambda =$\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "To find $\\lambda ,$ we want to match the expression\n\n$$\nc=\\lambda a+2b.\n$$\n\nWe are given that one of the components is $2b.$ So, we first draw the vector $2b$ from the origin. Then we complete the path to the point $C.$\n\n![](images/8d/8daf3d00afbe4afe9678853f4fec50dd0861ca9a36a6dae96f8fa2d288c45be1.png)\n\nLet $P$ be the endpoint of the vector $2b.$ Then\n\n$$\nc=\\overset{\\to }{OP}+\\overset{\\to }{PC}.\n$$\n\nIn $△OPC,$ we have:\n\n- $PC=2|a|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{PC}$ and $a$ have opposite directions\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{PC}=-2a.$\n\nAdditionally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c & =\\overset{\\to }{OP}+\\overset{\\to }{PC} \\\\  & =2b-2a.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $c=\\lambda a+2b=-2a+2b.$\n\nSince $a∦b,$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients